WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.669 --> 00:00:07.000
Sleep actually enhances your memory and
it refreshes your learning ability.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.240
You need sleep after learning to essentially
hit the save button on those new

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.127
memories, so that you don't forget.

00:00:17.127 --> 00:00:19.500
But what we've also learned is that you need

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.810
sleep before learning as well and now -to 
almost prepare your brain, a little bit

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:29.609
like a dry sponge ready to initially
soak up new information. So you need

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:33.750
sleep on both sides of that equation and
that's why pulling the all-nighter

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.280
before the exam is a very bad idea.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.489
When you sleep you actually see almost a

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:50.489
three-fold increase in creative
abilities as a consequence and there's

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:55.050
some wonderful anecdotes demonstrating
this sleep inspired creative benefit.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.789
Keith Richards, from the Rolling Stones,
actually used to go to sleep with a

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:04.559
guitar and a tape recorder because he
understood the power of dream sleep, what

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:10.229
we call REM sleep, and he describes in
his autobiography how one night he

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:14.760
started the tape recorder, 
went to sleep and the next morning the

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.810
tape had run all the way through, he rewinded it back and there in some ghostly

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.800
vision were the opening chords of
Satisfaction,

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.000
the most famous Rolling Stones song.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.200
And then he said it was followed by about 43 minutes of snoring thereafter.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.560
It's probably the reason that
no one has ever told you

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.000
to stay awake on a problem.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:46.040
Sleep is a wonderful health panacea. Every major disease that seems to be

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.210
killing us in the developed world has
significant -and many of them causal-

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.287
links to a lack of sleep. So the two most
feared diseases,

00:01:53.287 --> 00:01:57.100
which are Alzheimer's disease and cancer, both have strong links

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.900
to short sleep duration across the lifespan.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.009
Short sleep will actually
predict all cause mortality. So you may

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:14.500
have heard that old maxim people would
tell you that "you can sleep when you're dead".

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:19.000
Well, it is mortally unwise advice if you adopt that mindset.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.820
We know from the
science that you will be both dead sooner

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.600
and the quality of that now
shorter life will be significantly worse.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.019
Less sleep does not equal more
productivity, it's a fallacy that still

00:02:36.019 --> 00:02:39.440
remains in business and it's actually a
very costly one too.  The RAND Corporation

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.120
several years ago performed a global
survey of the cost of sleep deprivation

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.620
across nations and what they found was
that insufficient sleep within the

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:54.739
workplace cost most nations about two
percent of their GDP. So, just think about

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:59.120
that, if we could solve the sleep crisis
within the workplace we could perhaps

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.310
double the budget for education, maybe we could even half the healthcare deficit

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.359
that were suffering in most of these
developed nations. So sound sleep is

00:03:08.359 --> 00:03:12.000
sound business, that's exactly what the
science teaches us.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.000
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

